From 6d17b5a98af05c4b220414cf7275738cbbc55b16 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Stew Forster <ci@rdna-boosts.invalid>
Date: Mon, 16 Nov 2026 23:43:27 -0600
Subject: [PATCH 16/18] rdna-boosts: block 16: the server's prompt admission
 policy -- max-min fair share of the step, an interactive batch size (-ib)
 that applies only while a decoder waits, a shortest-remaining skew, the
 prefill/decode duty cycle, and the KV admission of a whole task

A batch is assembled once per step and cannot be joined mid-flight, so the policy that
decides WHICH prompt tokens enter that step also decides how long every other slot waits.

  * LLAMA_SERVER_PREFILL_FAIR (a kill-switch, default on) gives the slots that still have
    prompt tokens a max-min fair share of the window: every active prompt is admitted to
    every step, and a slot that needs less returns the difference to the pool.
  * LLAMA_SERVER_PREFILL_SKEW (default 1 = the plain share) weights the slot with the
    shortest remaining prompt N:1 against the others; every other slot still advances.
  * -ib / --interactive-batch-size (r306; env LLAMA_ARG_INTERACTIVE_BATCH_SIZE) caps the
    prompt tokens admitted per step WHILE a decoder is waiting -- the interactive bound on
    a decoding slot's inter-token latency and on a newcomer's wait for admission.  -1 = one
    n_ubatch (the default), 0 = no bound, N = explicit.  A step with NOTHING decoding takes
    the whole -b window instead (r306): with no decoder waiting the cap bounds nothing, and
    below -ub a smaller step only re-pays the step's fixed cost for nothing.  It was
    LLAMA_SERVER_PREFILL_CHUNK, env-only and applied to EVERY step, which cost the field
    shape half of its uncontended prefill (1454 vs 3036 t/s on the same 65536 tokens); that
    env var is kept as the option's deprecated alias, with one WARN.
  * LLAMA_SERVER_PREFILL_DUTY (default 16) leaves up to K-1 steps decode-only and puts the
    prompt into one step per cycle, BOUNDED in time by the previous prefill step's own
    duration, so a decoder gets a burst at the decode-only step time instead of one token
    per slow step.

New at r365 (contributor PR #370, briansp2020): the same policy now also decides whether a
TASK is admitted at all, because with a unified KV cache -- the delivery's DEFAULT server,
since -np -1 resolves to 4 slots and kv_unified = true -- every slot may use the whole
context, so "a slot is free" says nothing about whether the prompt fits.  Two prompts whose
sum exceeded the context were launched together and the failure landed in DECODE, where
nothing can recover it: the retry loop can only clear IDLE slots (try_clear_idle_slots skips
a processing one), so it halved n_batch to 1 and aborted a running slot (measured on r364 at
-c 8192 -np 4 --kv-unified with two ~4.8k-token prompts: both HTTP 500 "Context size has
been exceeded", and with a speculator "speculative batch index N is not inside the current
sub-batch"; the same two prompts alone: both 200).

  * server_slot_sched gains an injected `admit` oracle (std::function<bool(const
    server_task &)>) and server_slot_plan a `why` (none / no_slot / no_capacity).  select()
    asks the oracle ONCE, before a candidate is chosen, so the predicate is a whole-request
    go/no-go and the scheduler learns nothing about cells, reserves or margins; a refusal
    travels the existing "plan.id_slot == -1 -> queue_tasks.defer()" path, so there is no
    second defer site, pop_deferred_task() and /metrics' n_tasks_deferred cover it for free,
    and a prompt pinned to an idle id_slot is refused by the same path.
  * The server context owns the accounting (kv_admit_ok): each RUNNING slot is charged
    max(its prompt, its task's tokens) plus a reply reserve.  Idle slots are not charged (the
    launch path parks the residents and then clears them for want of room) and neither is a
    realtime slot waiting for audio (is_processing() but no batch work).  With nothing
    running a task is always admitted, so a prompt that fits the context alone is never
    starved.
  * LLAMA_SERVER_KV_ADMIT_RESERVE (default min(n_ctx / n_parallel, n_ctx_slot()) cells, the
    per-sequence share a non-unified cache would give each slot) is the reply space charged
    per request, capped by the request's own n_predict.  LLAMA_SERVER_KV_ADMIT=0 restores
    the pre-r365 server exactly.  Inert without --kv-unified.
  * A release wakes exactly one deferred task, so a task that is re-offered and refused
    again would wait for an unrelated request: update_slots() re-offers one when NO slot is
    processing -- the condition that makes the wake provably spin-free, since the popped
    task is then always admitted.
  * A task that was never launched is answered at termination: server_context::terminate()
    drains the deferred queue (server_queue::take_deferred_tasks) and aborts the running
    slots.  Without it a SIGTERM with a deferred task pending never returned -- the exit
    hung in HIP's teardown because server_http_context::join() waits for an HTTP worker
    waiting on a result nothing produced.  PRE-EXISTING (the stock "no free slot" deferral
    escaped it because the release path launches the task and then aborts it).

Measured (r365, one R9700, 4B Qwen3.5 Q8_0, -c 8192 -np 4 --kv-unified): two copies of
prompts/prose-rdna-boosts.txt (5246 tokens, cache_prompt false) answer 500/500 on
LLAMA_SERVER_KV_ADMIT=0 and 200/200 with it, at 2.1 s then 4.0 s (serialised), and BOTH
answers are 1dfd5ced6f3e -- the same text the prompt gives when it runs alone, the deferred
one re-prefilling all 5246 tokens.  A pair that fits is untouched (no deferral, text equal
to the kill-switch arm), four overlapping prompts drain in order, the reserve census reads
2048 at -c 8192 / 4096 at -c 16384 / 256 with LLAMA_SERVER_KV_ADMIT_RESERVE=256 / +n_predict
when the request caps it, a non-unified server defers nothing, SIGTERM with three pending
deferrals exits in 0.1 s (it never returned before), and a waiting task adds no CPU while
the server idles at 0 ticks after the drain.  Gate: scripts/gate-kv-admit.sh (MANIFESTS.md
gate 46), whose --self-test runs every arm on the kill switch and rejects it on 12 checks.
Knobs: ENVIRONMENT.md 29 / DIAGNOSTIC.md 29.  Record: WORKLOG.md 261126.001, INVARIANTS.md
r365, patches/README.md block 16.
